"""Serialize hand-authored claim and problem dossiers after complete source reading."""
import json,csv
from pathlib import Path
OUT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
claims=[];problems=[]
PRO='sources/texts/pro/MPL_68_033-056+162-1+162-6_transcription.tex'
G1='sources/texts/codex/K161-6-01_transcription.tex'
G2='sources/texts/codex/K161-6-02_transcription.tex'
CODE='parts/misc/computations/check_misc.py'
OUTPUT='parts/misc/computations/check_misc_output.txt'
def c(items,cote,scans,lines,his,statement,status,proof,cls,fate,refs,confidence='high',calc=False):
    n=len(claims)+1
    obj=dict(id=f'C-SA23-misc-{n:03d}',items=[f'SA23-{i}' for i in items],cote=cote,scans=scans,lines=lines,his_statement=his,statement=statement,status_on_pages=status,computation={'code':CODE if calc else None,'output':OUTPUT if calc else None,'proof_or_check':proof,'limit':'Finite calculations certify the stated instances only; general arguments are stated separately.' if calc else 'Analytic reasoning, not a machine certificate.'},**{'class':cls},fate=fate,fate_date='Dates of matched sources are in references.bib; the sheets are undated and no first-publication priority is inferred.',present_status='As of 19 November 2026: '+proof,refs=refs,confidence=confidence)
    claims.append(obj);return obj['id']
def p(title,occurrences,he,today,art,why,worth,refs):
    problems.append(dict(id=f'MP-SA23-misc-{len(problems)+1:02d}',title=title,occurrences=occurrences,as_he_states=he,plausible_today=today,state_of_art=art,why_open=why,worth=worth,refs=refs))

c([303],['68'],[33],[PRO+':2–43'],'G iff a successor is P; P iff every successor is G, for nonterminal positions.','For a well-founded two-player move graph with separately specified terminal outcomes, define winning and losing positions recursively, restricting the successor clauses to nonterminals.','stated without proof','Well-founded recursion gives existence and uniqueness. At a nonterminal a winning move exists precisely when a losing successor exists; universal winning successors give a losing position. Terminal winners are independent boundary data.','B','AptSimon2021 Theorem14 establishes determinacy for well-founded win/lose games; the three-outcome extension is Theorem17. Exact terminal convention is reconstructed here.',['AptSimon2021'])
c([304,305],['68'],[34],[PRO+':45–66'],'Conditions1–4 imply Gtilde subset G and Ptilde subset P.','Under no infinite plays, the four displayed sufficient conditions imply the stated inclusions, hence equality when both proposed and actual pairs are complementary.','proof sketched','Choose a minimal-rank counterexample. For a proposed winner the required losing successor contradicts minimality; for a proposed loser all successors are winners. The stronger literal condition1 excludes terminal proposed winners but does not invalidate sufficiency.','B','Well-founded induction is the relevant modern result, not a maximum finite game length; compare AptSimon2021 §2 and Theorem14.',['AptSimon2021'])
c([304],['68'],[34],[PRO+':50;68'],'Gtilde=G and Ptilde=P iff conditions1–4.','The stated necessity fails when a terminal position may be winning and condition1 quantifies over all proposed winners.','false as stated','Counterexample C={t}, R(t)=empty, G={t}, P=empty satisfies well-foundedness, terminal data and both complement conditions. Equality holds; condition1 fails. Repair condition1 to x in Gtilde minus terminal positions.','C','Corrected terminal recursion matches standard well-founded games; no source validating the literal false clause was found.',['AptSimon2021'])
c([307,308],['68'],[48],[PRO+':505–558'],'Successor permutation of players determined by j and R.','For an N-player move system, if every player labels a position with an outgoing move, at most one scheduler rho satisfies j(y)=rho(j(x)) for all moves x to y. A cyclic-permutation requirement is additional structure. The page defines no payoff equilibrium theorem.','stated without proof','If each player labels a position with an outgoing move, one equation determines each scheduler value. Surjectivity of j alone permits all positions terminal and supplies no equations. A scheduler may nonetheless be uniquely forced in special small cases; no unnecessary converse is asserted.','B','AptSimon2021 studies extensive games with explicit payoffs; it does not turn these move-system definitions into an equilibrium theorem.',['AptSimon2021'])
c([309],['68'],[36],[PRO+':78–87'],'exp:N(A)→1+N(A) is bijective, inverse log.','In any associative unital Q-algebra, finite exponential and logarithm give inverse bijections between nilpotent elements and unipotent units.','stated without proof','Formal one-variable identities descend modulo x^m. exp(x)−1=x times a polynomial with constant coefficient1, and log(1+x)=x times such a polynomial, so nilpotence exponents are preserved. The script verifies truncations through degree8.','B','Milne2017 §14e supplies the established characteristic-zero exponential framework; the arbitrary-associative-algebra quantifiers are re-proved here.',['Milne2017'],calc=True)
c([309],['68'],[36],[PRO+':88–96'],'Commuting x,y give exp(x+y)=exp(x)exp(y), and log(uv)=log(u)+log(v).','Commuting nilpotents have nilpotent sum; commuting unipotents have unipotent product; finite exp and log carry these two laws into one another.','stated without proof','If x^r=y^s=0 and xy=yx, every monomial of total degree r+s−1 vanishes. Binomial coefficients prove the exponential identity; apply its inverse to commuting logarithms. The literal exp1=0 is impossible and is retained as a reading alarm.','B','Milne2017 §14e records the commuting identity; finite-series proof covers the stated Q-algebra case.',['Milne2017'],calc=True)
c([309],['68'],[36],[PRO+':97–100'],'Power-stable Q-subspace L of nilpotents maps bijectively onto1+L.','For a Q-linear subspace L consisting of nilpotents and closed under positive powers, exp and log restrict to inverse maps L↔1+L.','stated without proof','Each relevant finite polynomial has zero constant coefficient and lies in L by linearity and power stability. No common nilpotence bound on L is needed.','B','Finite polynomial calculus is the exact mechanism; Milne2017 provides related exponential context, not this unrestricted statement as a quoted theorem.',['Milne2017'])
c([309],['68'],[36],[PRO+':101–102'],'1+L is a group; transported law commutative si|ssi L commutative; réciproque?','If L is a Q-subalgebra consisting of nilpotents,1+L is a group. Its group law is commutative iff xy=yx for all x,y in L. In that case exp identifies addition with multiplication.','open on the pages','Closure follows from x+y+xy in L and inverse1−x+x²−…. If1+L commutes, (1+x)(1+y)=(1+y)(1+x) directly gives xy=yx. If the marginal converse instead means exp(x+y)=exp(x)exp(y) for all x,y, replace x,y by tx,sy and compare ts to get [x,y]=0. This is a universal identity, not a claim about one isolated pair.','B','The elementary converse is settled by direct algebra. Milne2017 Cor14.33 covers the commutative unipotent finite-type setting; no manuscript-priority attribution is made.',['Milne2017'])
c([310],['68'],[36],[PRO+':104–111'],'For nil ideal J of central commutative K, JA consists of nilpotents.','If K is commutative, A a unital K-algebra and every element of J is nilpotent, each finite sum in JA is nilpotent, even if J has no uniform nilpotence exponent.','stated without proof','Write z=sum j_i a_i. The finite central ideal generated by j_i is nilpotent: if j_i^{e_i}=0, every product of more than sum(e_i−1) coefficients vanishes. Hence a corresponding power of z is zero, despite noncommuting a_i.','B','Matched to nilpotent coefficient algebra rather than globally nilpotent derivations; standard finite-generation argument given in full here.',['Milne2017'])
c([310],['68'],[36],[PRO+':112–131'],'J nilpotent; endomorphisms with image JM exponentiate to identity-mod-J automorphisms.','If J^r=0 in commutative K and M is any K-module, the ideal L={u:uM⊂JM} of End_K(M) satisfies L^r=0 and exp:L→1+L is a bijection.','stated without proof','K-linearity gives u(J^iM)⊂J^{i+1}M. Every product of r members of L is zero. The inserted nilpotent condition, as opposed to merely nil, is essential to this uniform argument.','B','Finite nilpotent-ideal exponential calculus applies; this is distinct from the later false derivation-into-an-internal-ideal claim.',['Milne2017'])
c([312],['68'],[37,38],[PRO+':137–238'],'U→Homgr(formal additive group,units of U⊗−) is bijective.','For a unital associative algebra U over a commutative Q-algebra K, natural group morphisms from the nilpotent-scalar formal additive group to units of U⊗K− are exactly λ↦exp(λu), u∈U.','proof sketched','Evaluate on K[t]/t^n and pass through the compatible finite coefficients. The group law imposes (i+j)!a_{i+j}=i!j!a_i a_j, hence a_n=u^n/n!. Naturality gives the converse on every nilpotent test scalar. No projectivity of U is needed.','B','Milne2017 treats representable algebraic groups; the formal universal-ring argument here verifies the larger stated functor category rather than assuming its hypotheses from that book.',['Milne2017'],calc=True)
c([312],['68'],[37,38],[PRO+':137–238'],'N(U)→Homgr(Ga,units of U⊗−) is bijective.','The analogous ordinary additive-group morphisms are parametrized by nilpotent u∈U.','proof sketched','Evaluate the natural transformation at universal t∈K[t]. Its image is an actual element of U[t], with finite support. The same recurrence gives u^n/n!, forcing global nilpotence. Conversely nilpotence gives a polynomial inverse exp(−tu).','B','Exact quantifiers verified by Yoneda and finite coefficients; compare the characteristic-zero one-parameter framework in Milne2017.',['Milne2017'])
c([313],['68'],[39],[PRO+':242–276'],'For arbitrary M, coefficients u_n vanish except finitely many; N(End M) gives every Ga action.','On the base-change module functor W(M), ordinary Ga actions correspond to locally nilpotent endomorphisms. A uniform exponent is not available for arbitrary M.','false as stated','M=Q[x],D=d/dx gives m(x)↦m(x+t). Every vector has a finite polynomial orbit, while D^n(x^n)=n!≠0 for every n. Formal actions still correspond to arbitrary endomorphisms by coefficientwise evaluation.','C','Milne2017 Theorem4.8 gives locally finite rational representations over fields; DuboulozLiendo2014 introduction recalls the Ga/LND correspondence for affine coordinate rings. Their hypotheses do not justify a uniform exponent on arbitrary M.',['Milne2017','DuboulozLiendo2014'])
c([313],['68'],[39],[PRO+':266–276'],'NB M finite projective gives the expected identifications.','If M is finitely generated over K, a locally nilpotent endomorphism is uniformly nilpotent. Finite projectivity additionally identifies End(M)⊗A with End_A(M⊗A).','stated without proof','Choose finitely many generators and take the largest exponent killing them. For finite projectivity use M*=Hom(M,K) and End(M)=M*⊗M. The more general finitely generated uniform-nilpotence statement is our reconstruction.','B','The finite-dimensional representation setting of Milne2017 is a special case; the projective base-change calculation is independent.',['Milne2017'])
c([314,318],['68'],[40,41,42],[PRO+':280–357'],'fu=vf iff f exp(u)=exp(v) f, and iff equivariance.','For nilpotent u,v, intertwining is equivalent before and after exponential and for their formal/ordinary one-parameter actions. The statement also extends pointwise to locally nilpotent endomorphisms.','proof sketched','Forward direction applies the power series. Reverse direction applies finite log to exp(u),exp(v), or coefficient of t for full actions. The written delta proof needs Hom(M,N) and c_ij=−1/(i+j+1)!, i+j≥1. For locally nilpotent operators each vector gives a finite log identity, with no global cutoff required.','B','Characteristic-zero exponential naturality is established; the exact proof repairs and pointwise extension are checked here.',['Milne2017'],calc=True)
c([315],['68'],[42],[PRO+':359–373'],'Tensor infinitesimal operator u⊗1+1⊗v.','The tensor product of two additive-group representations has infinitesimal generator u⊗1+1⊗v.','stated without proof','The two tensor summands commute; multiplying their exponentials gives exp(tu)⊗exp(tv). Uniform nilpotence follows from bounds for u,v; local nilpotence follows for each finite sum of simple tensors.','B','Standard tensor compatibility of representations; the coefficient calculation is exact.',['Milne2017'],calc=True)
c([315],['68'],[42,43],[PRO+':374–385'],'Algebra automorphisms correspond to derivations, with no associativity or unit assumption.','For a K-bilinear multiplication μ, exp(tu) preserves μ iff uμ=μ(u⊗1+1⊗u). For nilpotent u, exp(u) preserving μ already implies this derivation equation.','proof sketched','The full action statement is coefficient comparison. For one exponential use the intertwiner theorem on μ between the tensor generator and u; tensor nilpotence is needed. The argument permits nonassociative, nonunital multiplication exactly as written.','B','The general multilinear-equivariance proof is checked here; DuboulozLiendo2014 covers the familiar commutative affine case.',['DuboulozLiendo2014','Milne2017'])
c([316],['68'],[43],[PRO+':382–386'],'Hom differential vf−fu, but representability requires care.','The internal Hom action has generator f↦vf−fu when the internal Hom base-change functor is represented by the indicated module; finite projectivity of the source suffices.','stated without proof','Conjugation gives exp(tv)f exp(−tu). The canonical Hom(M,N)⊗A→Hom_A(M⊗A,N⊗A) is not generally bijective. Explicit K=Q[e]/e²,M=K⊕Q base-change map has rank3 between four-dimensional Q-spaces.','B','The representation construction is established, while the source explicitly flags the representability caveat. The finite counterexample verifies why it matters.',['Milne2017'],calc=True)
c([317],['68'],[43,44],[PRO+':388–416'],'Are both exponential parametrizations bijective for all semilinear module-valued functors?','Without restrictions on a natural module-valued functor on K-algebras, neither the formal injectivity claim nor the ordinary surjectivity claim follows.','open on the pages','For M(A)=A_red over Q, natural endomorphisms0,id differ but exp(λid)=id whenever λ is nilpotent. Thus the formal parametrization is not injective. For W(Q[x]), translations show ordinary surjectivity from uniformly nilpotent endomorphisms fails.','C','Sources on representable groups and Ga-actions do not assert the unrestricted functor statement. These two counterexamples settle the question in its literal generality.',['Milne2017','DuboulozLiendo2014'])
c([319,320],['68'],[44],[PRO+':427–434'],'A locally nilpotent sheaf derivation exponentiates, fixing the reduced scheme.','For a sheaf derivation on a characteristic-zero scheme that is locally nilpotent on stalks in the required compatible sheaf sense, pointwise finite exponential gives a sheaf automorphism with identity reduction. Commuting such operators compose by exp(Θ+Θ′)=expΘ∘expΘ′.','stated without proof','In a reduced local Q-algebra, exp(tD) sends units to units of A[t], whose nonconstant coefficients are nilpotent and hence vanish. D vanishes on units; every a or a−1 is a unit, so D=0. Derivations preserve nilradicals in characteristic0. Affine-ring local nilpotence alone is insufficient for stalkwise nilpotence: d/dx on Q[x] does not stay locally nilpotent on Q[x,x−1].','C','DuboulozLiendo2014 Theorem2.1 distinguishes rational integrability and global local nilpotence for semi-affine varieties. It is related, not the same sheafwise hypothesis.',['DuboulozLiendo2014'])
c([321],['68'],[45],[PRO+':437–459'],'u differential order≤n iff J^{n+1}=0 iff identity modulo an ideal of exponent n+1.','For a commutative unital Q-algebra A and an automorphism u, J=(u(a)−a:a∈A) controls its differential order exactly: ord(u)≤n iff J^{n+1}=0.','stated without proof','[u,m_a]=m_{u(a)−a}u. An (n+1)-fold commutator is multiplication by a product of n+1 differences followed by u. Since u(1)=1, vanishing is equivalent to vanishing of every generator product of J^{n+1}. The ideal-I formulation is equivalent to J⊂I.','B','Stacks09CH Definition10.133.1 provides the exact recursive definition; EGAIV4 Definition16.8.1 pp39–40 and Proposition16.8.8 pp42–43 give the principal-parts and commutator definitions; the exact automorphism criterion is independently deduced here.',['Stacks09CH','EGAIV4'])
c([322],['68'],[45],[PRO+':446'],'u induces identity on gr_J(A).','Identity modulo the difference ideal does not imply identity on its associated graded algebra.','false as stated','A=Q[e]/e²,u(e)=2e is a unital Q-algebra automorphism, J=(e),J²=0. On J/J² it acts by2, contradicting the live NB.','C','The differential-operator framework remains valid; the stronger associated-graded assertion is refuted without extra hypotheses.',['Stacks09CH'],calc=True)
c([323],['68'],[45],[PRO+':460–465'],'It suffices that (u−id)^{n+1}=0, equivalently u=expΘ with Θ^{n+1}=0.','Global unipotence of exponent n+1 implies the differential-order bound n for a commutative Q-algebra automorphism.','corrected by himself later (both places)','The author replaces the earlier (iv) equivalence at460 with sufficiency and asks necessity at465. A separate rigorous proof: for D^m=0, apply D^m to a^r for1≤r≤m, multiply by a^{m−r}, and triangularly eliminate lower falling-factorial terms to obtain(Da)^m=0; polarization gives(D(A))^m=0. For D=log u, the difference ideal equals the ideal generated by D(A). Here m=n+1.','B','The correct sufficiency is proved in this run; Stacks09CH then gives the order bound. Necessity is false as C-SA23-misc-025 records.',['Stacks09CH','Milne2017'])
c([323],['68'],[45],[PRO+':469–474'],'exp bijects nilpotent derivations of bound n+1 and automorphisms with (u−id)^{n+1}=0.','Finite exponential and logarithm preserve the nilpotence exponent and identify globally nilpotent derivations with globally unipotent algebra automorphisms, over Q.','stated without proof','For u unipotent, u^t=sum binomial(t,j)(u−1)^j is polynomial in t and is multiplicative for every integer t. A polynomial over a Q-vector space vanishing at all integers is zero, so differentiating at0 gives log(u) a derivation. Conversely the iterated Leibniz rule gives exp(D) multiplicative. Both exponent bounds are preserved by polynomial factors with constant coefficient1.','B','Characteristic-zero exponential theory corroborates the restricted statement; the general Q-algebra argument is supplied here.',['Milne2017'],calc=True)
c([323],['68'],[45],[PRO+':475–485'],'All differential automorphisms are exponentials of nilpotent derivations.','The lower corollary reasserts an equivalence withdrawn above; it is false even for the dual numbers.','false as stated','For A=Q[e]/e²,u(e)=2e, the first commutator is nonzero and all second commutators vanish, so order1. But u−id has eigenvalue1 on e and is not nilpotent. Exponential of a nilpotent operator is unipotent, so no nilpotent derivation exponentiates to u. All algebra, characteristic and invertibility hypotheses hold.','C','Corrected finite exponential correspondence C-SA23-misc-024 is established; it does not cover all differential automorphisms. No published source found validates the false equivalence.',['Stacks09CH','Milne2017'],calc=True)
c([324],['68'],[46],[PRO+':489–491'],'If nilradical nilpotent, previous conditions equivalent to u_red=id.','For nilpotent nilradical N(A), an automorphism is differential iff it acts identically on A_red. This is not equivalent to being the exponential of a globally nilpotent derivation.','stated without proof','If u is differential then its difference ideal is nilpotent and lies in N(A). Conversely u_red=id gives J⊂N(A), so J has the nilpotence exponent of N(A). The word previous includes a false earlier (iv); only(i)–(iii) extend.','C','Stacks09CH plus the exact difference-ideal criterion supplies the restricted equivalence.',['Stacks09CH'])
c([324],['68'],[46],[PRO+':493'],'Every differential operator has order≤N when N(A)^{N+1}=0.','A nilradical exponent bounds these identity-on-reduction automorphisms, not arbitrary differential operators.','false as stated','Take A=Q[t],N(A)=0,N=0. D=d/dt satisfies[D,m_t]=id and has exact order1. Thus the universal written sentence is false, even though the immediately preceding statement about(u,N) is correct.','C','Stacks09CH Example10.133.5 explicitly includes derivations among first-order operators. EGAIV4 Theorem16.11.2 p54 gives the divided-power differential-operator basis under differential smoothness, and16.12.2 p55 gives the characteristic-zero realization; the source text, not a repaired wording, is indexed.',['Stacks09CH','EGAIV4'])
c([325],['68'],[46],[PRO+':495'],'u K-automorphism iff Θ K-derivation.','When u=expΘ and Θ is a nilpotent Q-derivation on a K-algebra, u fixes K iff Θ vanishes on K.','stated without proof','Forward direction uses Θ=log(u): every positive power of(u−1) kills K. Reverse direction uses expΘ. This is K-linearity, not A-linearity.','B','The finite exp/log formalism proves the relative assertion exactly.',['Milne2017'])
c([326],['68'],[46],[PRO+':497–503'],'Derivations with image I satisfy Θ^iA⊂I^i and exponentiate bijectively to all identity-mod-I automorphisms.','For an arbitrary internal nilpotent ideal I, image in I does not imply filtration raising or nilpotence. A sufficient corrected condition is Θ(A)⊂I and Θ(I)⊂I², paired with automorphisms acting identically on gr_I.','false as stated','A=Q[e]/e²,I=(e),Θ(e)=e is a Q-derivation with image I but Θ^m(e)=e for every m. u(e)=2e is identity modulo I but not unipotent. With filtration raising, induction gives Θ(I^r)⊂I^{r+1}; exp/log preserve that property and the finite inverse argument applies.','C','Finite unipotent correspondence is established, but it requires the additional filtration condition here. This differs from the valid central base-ideal module example C-SA23-misc-010.',['Milne2017','Stacks09CH'],calc=True)
c([327],['68'],[50],[PRO+':562–578'],'Two-element set and contracted-product lemmas.','For two-element torsors, bijective maps are exactly involution-equivariant maps; a binary map induces an isomorphism of the contracted product iff both slices are bijections with the required involution relation.','stated without proof','All4 unary and16 binary maps are enumerated; exactly2 in each class give torsor isomorphisms. Direct proof chooses one element in each torsor and uses involution to force all remaining values.','B','These elementary torsor facts underlie determinant-orientation constructions. No historical priority claimed; the exact finite assertions are independently verified.',['KnudsenMumford1976'],calc=True)
c([328],['68'],[50],[PRO+':580–615'],'Equivalent orientation axioms using boundary maps and contracted products.','A natural family of bijective Stokes maps for choices of a half-space, reversed by the opposite half-space, is equivalent to the corresponding isomorphism of two-element torsors.','proof sketched','Apply the preceding contracted-product lemma separately to every hyperplane and use its uniqueness to verify naturality. Cardinality2 alone does not replace the required isomorphism.','B','The determinant-line construction supplies an established model; equivalence of these exact axioms is a direct finite torsor argument.',['KnudsenMumford1976'])
c([329],['68'],[50],[PRO+':617–642'],'Every morphism of orientation theories is an isomorphism; there is a unique isomorphism; Existence?','Normalized orientation theories have at most one morphism and any morphism is invertible. Existence of a morphism and existence of a theory are distinct questions, solved by a determinant-sign model with compatible half-space maps.','open on the pages','Dimension induction with a hyperplane proves invertibility and uniqueness once the dimension0 normalization is fixed. The page marks both existence steps. A determinant quotient(det V minus0)/R_{>0}, with ordered normal convention, supplies existence; identification of the characters makes the normalized maps natural.','B','Classical determinant-line orientations give the concrete model; KnudsenMumford1976 supplies the graded determinant formalism, not this exact axiomatization as a quoted theorem.',['KnudsenMumford1976'])
c([330],['68'],[52],[PRO+':691–734'],'ε_n(u)=sign det(u).','Compatible orientation characters on GL_n(R), normalized in degree0 and satisfying the half-space stabilizer rule, are forced to be sign(det).','proof sketched','The displayed rule handles a parabolic stabilizer. Elementary shears have sign1 by conjugacy into such a stabilizer, diagonal matrices have the product of their one-dimensional signs, and Gaussian elimination generates GL_n(R) from shears and diagonals. Thus the omitted extension to the whole group is valid.','B','Determinant-sign orientation is established; the missing generation step is independently checked.',['KnudsenMumford1976'])
c([333,335],['68'],[54],[PRO+':810–813'],'Exchanging orientation factors multiplies by(−1)^{dim V dim V′}.','On the ordered direct-sum orientation product, exchanging dimensions p,q contributes the Koszul sign(−1)^{pq}.','stated without proof','Moving p basis vectors past q others uses pq transpositions. Two independent boundary normal lines anticommute, which is the local corner-cancellation sign. The chain-complex interpretation is reconstruction, not an assertion on this sheet.','B','KnudsenMumford1976 p20 states the exact graded-line symmetry.',['KnudsenMumford1976'],calc=True)
c([331,334],['68'],[54],[PRO+':837–848'],'St(ω×ω′)=(−1)^q St(ω)×ω′; other factor has no sign.','With the manuscript-inferred inward-normal-last convention, taking the boundary in the first factor contributes(−1)^q; taking it in the second contributes no sign.','proof sketched','Move the normal past q vectors in the second factor. The basis calculation supports q but cannot settle the uncertain ink. Outward-normal-first differs in dimension n by(−1)^n, and normalized theory conversion is t_n=(−1)^{n(n+1)/2}.','B','The sign is the exact graded-line commutativity calculation; the convention comparison belongs to the earlier run and is rechecked here.',['KnudsenMumford1976'],calc=True)
c([332],['68'],[54],[PRO+':792–806'],'I(ω,ω′)∈Ω …','The flag U⊂W⊂V suggests a product orientation on V/U from W/U and V/W, but neither the full target nor an identity for I is written.','open on the pages','A quotient determinant isomorphism gives a plausible completion, with factor order determining the Koszul sign. This completion is not identifiable as the unique intended formula.','F','KnudsenMumford1976 p21 provides the short-exact-sequence determinant isomorphism, but does not identify this unfinished symbol.',['KnudsenMumford1976'],'medium')
c([336,339,340,341],['68'],[56],[PRO+':916–948'],'Axioms a,b for a theory of graded determinant modules on finite free modules.','A symmetric monoidal functor from the isomorphism groupoid of finite free k-modules to graded free lines, with rank-one normalization and direct-sum coherence, is the proposed data. No existence/uniqueness theorem is on the page.','stated without proof','Exterior powers give a model. Natural rank-one normalization is stronger than merely choosing an objectwise isomorphism: the weak reading permits(det V)^⊗3 in degree rank V over Q, whose scalar character is a³. Finite-projective extension must enlarge to all invertible lines and locally constant degree. Conditional K1-character twists remain only earlier-run constructions.','C','KnudsenMumford1976 pp20–21 develops graded invertible determinants; MuroTonksWitte2015 Definition1.2.3 uses more coherence. Its solution of a 1973 Grothendieck letter question does not establish uniqueness under this sheet’s abbreviated axioms.',['KnudsenMumford1976','MuroTonksWitte2015'])
c([337,338],['68'],[56],[PRO+':948–966'],'Complement independence iff Δ(u)=id for shears fixing W1 and V/W1.','For a functor with natural direct-sum tensor isomorphisms, independence of the free complement is equivalent to trivial action of every such block shear.','stated without proof','Choose two complements W2,W2′. Their quotient identifications produce a unique shear fixing W1 and carrying W2 to W2′; naturality transforms the independence square into Δ(shear)=id. Conversely use a complement and its shear image. The supplied square needs type/direction repairs; no ink decision is made.','B','The determinant of a block shear is1, so the standard determinant satisfies the criterion. Broader determinant-functor uniqueness remains separate.',['KnudsenMumford1976','MuroTonksWitte2015'])

def write():
    (OUT/'claims.json').write_text(json.dumps(claims,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
    (OUT/'problems.json').write_text(json.dumps(problems,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
    print('claims',len(claims),'problems',len(problems))
if __name__=='__main__':write()
